.he "mwformat (1)                 9/29/26                 mwformat (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
mwformat
- format a Morrow HD-DMA hard disk
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
mwformat [-c cyl] [-h heads] [-s spt] [-p precomp] [-l lowcur] [-d step]
[-k skew] [-m model] [-t track] [-e head] device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Mwformat lays down a hard disk's sector headers and data fields and writes
the disk label that says what geometry it laid them down as.  It runs the
controller's own commands - load the constants, recalibrate, step, format a
track - from the command line, one track at a time, printing a line as each
is done.  It is the work Morrow's FORMATMW did under CP/M, with the geometry
given here rather than looked up.
.sp
The device must be the drive's whole-disk slice, 'c' - hd0c for drive 0.
Block 0 of that slice is physical cylinder 0, head 0, sector 0 whatever the
geometry is, and that is where the label goes: it is the only address that
can be found before the label has been read, and the only way to open a disk
that has no label, which a disk being formatted does not.  label (1) needs
the same node for the same reason.
.sp
What the drive is comes from, in order: the flags below, the model named
with -m, the label the disk already carries, and failing all three, nothing
- a geometry that has to be stated, because the device node names a drive
and a slice and no geometry at all.  So a disk that already says what it is
needs no arguments, and a blank one needs -m or at least -c and -h.
.sp
A format blanks the disk: every sector header is rewritten and every data
field filled with 0xe5, so the label and any filesystem go with it.  The
label is written again from what was formatted, and mkfs (1) follows to make
a filesystem on it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.bd
-c cyl
.in +5
.br
Cylinders, or tracks per surface.  -h heads, -s sectors per track, -p the
cylinder where write precompensation begins, -l the cylinder where the write
current drops to the low setting, -d the step pulse delay in units of 100
microseconds.
.in -5
.bd
-m model
.in +5
.br
A drive this machine has nodes for: m5, m10, m16, m32 or m40.  The name is a
shorthand for the geometry and the timing together, and any of the flags
above overrides the part it names.
.in -5
.bd
-k skew
.in +5
.br
The interleave: the order the sectors of a track are laid down in, three by
default.  It is what a read header reports afterwards, so it is worth
leaving alone unless the drive needs a different one.
.in -5
.bd
-t track, -e head
.in +5
.br
Format only one cylinder, or only one head of every cylinder - a repair of
one track that will not read.  The label is rewritten when the walk crosses
cylinder 0 head 0, because the format has just destroyed the sector it lives
in; a walk that misses that sector leaves the label alone.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
.bd
mwformat -m m16 /dev/hd0c
.sp
mwformat -c 500 -h 8 -p 400 /dev/hd0c
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The select byte carries four head lines: three bits of it are the head
field, and the fourth is the low-current line, which an MFM drive with more
than eight heads uses as head select line 4.  Sixteen heads is therefore the
most this can lay down, and a drive that has more than eight is not told its
write current at all - that line is one of its head lines, and by then the
drive is doing its own current control.
.sp
The simulator does not model that line: it decodes three bits, so heads 8 and
up read back as head 0 there, and a disk with them is real hardware only.
.sp
The sector size is 512 bytes and is not an option.  FORMATMW formats at
1024 unless told otherwise and Micronix reads 512; a disk formatted at any
other size is a disk the kernel cannot read, so the gap after each sector
and the sector count that follow from the size are computed and not chosen.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
label (1), mkfs (1)
